{"page":642,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.33117,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"628 Chapter 10: Infinite Sequences and Series"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.08457,"w":0.59301,"h":0.03858,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"This is a geometric series with first term 1>2 and ratio r = -(x - 2)>2. It converges absolutely for \u0002 x - 2\u0002 6 2 and its sum is"},{"x":0.50936,"y":0.12798,"w":0.24638,"h":0.04173,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"1>2 1 + (x - 2)>2 = 1 2 + (x - 2) = 1 x."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.17282,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"In this example the Taylor series generated by ƒ(x) = 1>x at a = 2 converges to 1>x for"},{"x":0.32659,"y":0.19792,"w":0.19054,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"\u0002 x - 2\u0002 6 2 or 0 6 x 6 4."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.23533,"w":0.14181,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Taylor Polynomials"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.25997,"w":0.59299,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The linearization of a differentiable function ƒ at a point a is the polynomial of degree one given by"},{"x":0.5204,"y":0.29767,"w":0.19489,"h":0.01499,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P1(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.32136,"w":0.59305,"h":0.05885,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"In Section 3.11 we used this linearization to approximate ƒ(x) at values of x near a. If ƒ has derivatives of higher order at a, then it has higher-order polynomial approximations as well, one for each available derivative. These polynomials are called the Taylor polyno- mials of ƒ."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.42102,"w":0.53248,"h":0.05951,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"DEFINITION Let ƒ be a function with derivatives of order k for k = 1, 2, c , N in some interval containing a as an interior point. Then for any integer n from 0 through N, the Taylor polynomial of order n generated by ƒ at x = a is the polynomial"},{"x":0.3585,"y":0.48827,"w":0.24871,"h":0.02447,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Pn(x) = ƒ(a) + ƒ′(a)(x - a) + ƒ″(a)"},{"x":0.58366,"y":0.49328,"w":0.13074,"h":0.02556,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2! (x - a)2 + g"},{"x":0.55535,"y":0.52333,"w":0.05654,"h":0.02371,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"+ ƒ(k)(a)"},{"x":0.58582,"y":0.52333,"w":0.1972,"h":0.03131,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"k! (x - a)k + g + ƒ(n)(a)"},{"x":0.75615,"y":0.53282,"w":0.0893,"h":0.02182,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"n! (x - a)n."},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.563,"w":0.18578,"h":0.0201,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"3.0 y = P3(x)"},{"x":0.20178,"y":0.57277,"w":0.03545,"h":0.01216,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = e x"},{"x":0.24469,"y":0.59398,"w":0.05108,"h":0.01268,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = P2(x)"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.59498,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02957,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"We speak of a Taylor polynomial of order n rather than degree n because ƒ(n)(a) may be zero. The first two Taylor polynomials of ƒ(x) = cos x at x = 0, for example, are"},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.60075,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2.5"},{"x":0.32424,"y":0.62642,"w":0.55714,"h":0.01499,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P0(x) = 1 and P1(x) = 1. The first-order Taylor polynomial has degree zero, not one."},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.63835,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2.0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.64177,"w":0.59305,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Just as the linearization of ƒ at x = a provides the best linear approximation of ƒ in the neighborhood of a, the higher-order Taylor polynomials provide the “best” polynomial approximations of their respective degrees. (See Exercise 40.)"},{"x":0.23395,"y":0.64455,"w":0.05108,"h":0.01269,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = P1(x)"},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.67711,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.5"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.70981,"w":0.58813,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 2 Find the Taylor series and the Taylor polynomials generated by ƒ(x) = ex"},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.71188,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.72618,"w":0.06049,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"at x = 0."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.75229,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02957,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Solution Since ƒ(n)(x) = ex and ƒ(n)(0) = 1 for every n = 0, 1, 2, c, the Taylor series generated by ƒ at x = 0 (see Figure 10.17) is"},{"x":0.11502,"y":0.7529,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0.5"},{"x":0.52277,"y":0.78756,"w":0.21649,"h":0.03133,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"2! x2 + g + ƒ(n)(0) n! xn + g"},{"x":0.39694,"y":0.78831,"w":0.14938,"h":0.02296,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(0) + ƒ′(0)x + ƒ″(0)"},{"x":0.06818,"y":0.79694,"w":0.02726,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"−0.5"},{"x":0.13161,"y":0.79748,"w":0.06014,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 0.5"},{"x":0.22918,"y":0.79748,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.0"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.81917,"w":0.24905,"h":0.0128,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 10.17 The graph of ƒ(x) = ex"},{"x":0.7033,"y":0.82694,"w":0.09746,"h":0.02862,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"2 + g + xn"},{"x":0.61962,"y":0.82695,"w":0.09436,"h":0.02141,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"= 1 + x + x2"},{"x":0.78735,"y":0.83489,"w":0.06252,"h":0.02107,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"n! + g"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.8358,"w":0.15827,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and its Taylor polynomials"},{"x":0.05297,"y":0.85181,"w":0.08626,"h":0.01349,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"P1(x) = 1 + x"},{"x":0.05297,"y":0.86093,"w":0.15308,"h":0.02228,"fs":0.01261,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"P2(x) = 1 + x + (x2>2!)"},{"x":0.66124,"y":0.86403,"w":0.01257,"h":0.0142,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xk"},{"x":0.61973,"y":0.87197,"w":0.0368,"h":0.01695,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= a"},{"x":0.66118,"y":0.87266,"w":0.01849,"h":0.02038,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k!."},{"x":0.05297,"y":0.87884,"w":0.22512,"h":0.02228,"fs":0.01261,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"P3(x) = 1 + x + (x2>2!) + (x3>3!)."},{"x":0.63785,"y":0.88676,"w":0.02002,"h":0.00916,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k=0"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.90639,"w":0.58896,"h":0.02957,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"This is also the Maclaurin series for ex. In the next section we will see that the series con- verges to ex at every x."},{"x":0.04848,"y":0.9087,"w":0.24408,"h":0.02685,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Notice the very close agreement near the center x = 0 (Example 2)."}]}